[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
I investigated the PC0↔PC12 path and the OSPF backbone for instability and could not reproduce route flaps or periodic latency spikes.

Affected component: The suspected OSPF adjacency between r1 and r11 (area 0.0.0.3) is stable. EIGRP on r11 (used to learn 120.0.0.0/8 from r12 and redistribute into OSPF) is also stable.

Mechanism (why symptoms would appear if present): If the r1–r11 OSPF adjacency or the r11–r12 EIGRP adjacencies were flapping, r1 would repeatedly lose and relearn 120.0.0.0/8 (PC12’s network) as an OSPF external, causing route churn and ping jitter from PC0. This would show as frequent neighbor state resets and OSPF/EIGRP log events.

Localization evidence:
- r1 OSPF neighbors: Full with r3 (area 0) and r11 (area 3). “Most recent state change” just tracks initial formation (now >2 minutes), with 6 state changes typical of bring-up; no repeated resets. Dead timers counting down normally.
- r3 OSPF neighbors: All Full; same “last change” pattern.
- r11 OSPF neighbor to r1: Full/Backup; stable timers; no flaps.
- FRR logs (r1, r3, r11): single adjacency “Negotiation done” events; no down/up cycles afterward.
- r11 EIGRP neighbors (112.1.1.2=r12 and 103.1.1.2): Adjacent and stable; logs show “adjacency became full” once with no churn.
- Routing: r1 learns 120/8 via OSPF from r11; r11 learns 120/8 via EIGRP from r12; consistent on repeated checks.
- Performance: 50 ICMPs PC0→PC12 showed 0% loss, 0.75–1.53 ms RTT, no periodic spikes.

Root cause: No ongoing routing flaps detected; prior reports likely coincided with initial protocol convergence after lab start.

Corrective fix: None required. If concern persists, enable OSPF/EIGRP neighbor uptime logging and continuous latency monitoring to catch future anomalies. Current network is healthy and stable. 
=== END ===